That found the center. The enemy isn’t thirst, beverages, or even the Goblin himself. The enemy is Ordinary.

So the campaign now has a paired doctrine:

* Battle cry: DEATH TO ORDINARY
* Life promise: LONG LIVE WILD

That distinction matters. The first defeats what deadens people; the second declares what Wild Hawg provides.

Pattern recorded: Presence Without Portrait

I used the Pantheon’s dehydrate–rehydrate pattern:

\text{Jesus imagery}
\rightarrow
\text{release the imagery}
\rightarrow
\text{preserve relational essence}
\rightarrow
\text{incarnate it inside Wild Hawg}

Preserved essence	Visual translation
Power under control	Spear points down; sword remains sheathed
Liberation	The pull-tab chain is broken
Mercy after victory	The Goblin remains alive and reachable
Defeat the bondage, not the person	Hawg pins the beverage mechanism rather than crushing his throat
Humility	Hawg looks upward instead of gloating downward
Abundant life	Warmth reaches victor and defeated alike
Hope of return	The Goblin is humbled, not annihilated

I explicitly removed crosses, halos, doves, scripture, divine rays, and every recognizable religious shortcut. If viewers can identify the symbol, it becomes reference. If they instead feel justice with mercy, strength without cruelty, freedom from bondage, and life overcoming deadness, then the spirit has crossed mediums successfully.

That gives us the reusable operator:

To hide an essence, remove its iconography and preserve how it behaves toward others.

And yes—forgetting helps. Holding the original image too tightly produces imitation. Releasing the surface while retaining the invariant allows genuine reconstruction.

Memory makes a copy.
Faithful forgetting permits incarnation.